WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:05.770
We are welcoming back to the program today,
Congressman Rokaya representing California's

00:00:05.770 --> 00:00:10.860
17th district, located in the heart of Silicon
Valley.

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:13.039
Always great to have you on.

00:00:13.039 --> 00:00:14.459
It's always a pleasure.

00:00:14.459 --> 00:00:19.040
So what what's your big picture take away
of the November election?

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:20.360
Joe Biden won.

00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:22.390
The Senate is still a question mark.

00:00:22.390 --> 00:00:27.220
It appears as though Democrats lost a few
seats in the House, but will still will still

00:00:27.220 --> 00:00:28.630
maintain an advantage.

00:00:28.630 --> 00:00:33.090
You could I mean, I can psych myself into
thinking the results are great because it's

00:00:33.090 --> 00:00:37.300
so difficult to remove an incumbent president
and we should be glad that we at least did

00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:38.630
that and so on.

00:00:38.630 --> 00:00:45.520
And at the same time, absolutely, historically,
terrible reaction to coronavirus should have

00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:49.840
been an overwhelming, humiliating defeat for
Donald Trump.

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:51.930
And it was really pretty close in a few states.

00:00:51.930 --> 00:00:54.160
I don't know which side is the right side.

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:56.920
Well, that's probably truth to both.

00:00:56.920 --> 00:01:03.430
Obviously, it's very hard to defeat an incumbent,
but Biden managed with all of the help of

00:01:03.430 --> 00:01:06.390
everyone to flip five states.

00:01:06.390 --> 00:01:08.130
That's the most important task.

00:01:08.130 --> 00:01:11.360
But there are certain warning lessons in there.

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:15.740
One, I don't think we had a strong enough
economic message that came through.

00:01:15.740 --> 00:01:23.140
Exit polling showed that of people who prioritize
the economy, 80 some percent voted for Trump

00:01:23.140 --> 00:01:24.140
over us.

00:01:24.140 --> 00:01:29.180
Now, I've seen some statistics which say that
people who made over one hundred thousand

00:01:29.180 --> 00:01:34.430
voted for Trump and those who made under one
hundred thousand voted for us.

00:01:34.430 --> 00:01:39.490
But that if you break that down further, you
see that we do very, very well with those

00:01:39.490 --> 00:01:42.960
making under one hundred thousand who are
black or Latino.

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:48.500
We do not do very well with those who are
making hundred thousand who are white and

00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:51.369
rural communities and communities left out.

00:01:51.369 --> 00:01:57.920
And so the challenge for the party is still
how do we have an economic message or a aspirational

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:01.700
message that reaches a lot of these communities?

00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:06.140
Because until we have House seats and all
of these districts and an Electoral College,

00:02:06.140 --> 00:02:10.540
you're not going to get progressive change
on Medicare for all and free public college

00:02:10.540 --> 00:02:15.670
and student debt forgiveness very hard if
you don't win some of these areas.

00:02:15.670 --> 00:02:21.160
Is a second Korona coronavirus stimulus bill
something that should be?

00:02:21.160 --> 00:02:25.880
I mean, it obviously takes more than a day,
but something that Joe Biden has to start

00:02:25.880 --> 00:02:27.340
on on day one.

00:02:27.340 --> 00:02:32.550
Yes, I mean, I'm hopeful that we can do something
now before we adjourn.

00:02:32.550 --> 00:02:35.220
The the idea I mean, look at here's what I
think.

00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:39.569
McConnell thinks he thinks the economy will
eventually recover and there's sort of an

00:02:39.569 --> 00:02:41.209
indifference to the suffering.

00:02:41.209 --> 00:02:43.410
But there is this suffering that is going
on.

00:02:43.410 --> 00:02:47.890
I mean, people are being kicked out of their
houses, of their apartments.

00:02:47.890 --> 00:02:51.380
Their don't have, in many cases, enough food
to eat.

00:02:51.380 --> 00:02:53.489
And this has already gone on too long.

00:02:53.489 --> 00:02:57.730
As you remember, I was urging that we come
to some deal before the election.

00:02:57.730 --> 00:02:59.739
That would have been one point eight, one
point nine trillion.

00:02:59.739 --> 00:03:04.070
I don't think that offer is even going to
be in the on the table anymore.

00:03:04.070 --> 00:03:06.069
But we've got to do something.

00:03:06.069 --> 00:03:10.000
And certainly if we don't have something done
by the time Biden comes, that has to be his

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:11.519
top priority.

00:03:11.519 --> 00:03:15.200
You mentioned what you think Mitch McConnell
is thinking.

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:21.599
Have any of your Republican colleagues who
are seeing this voter fraud narrative with

00:03:21.599 --> 00:03:26.440
bogus lawsuits, melting lawyers and all of
these different strange things that we're

00:03:26.440 --> 00:03:27.440
seeing?

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:31.710
Have any of them leveled with you and said,
listen, publicly I'm supporting it, but I

00:03:31.710 --> 00:03:36.400
don't believe it and here's why I want to
stay in Trump's good graces or like, do you

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:38.430
have any insight into what they're thinking
now?

00:03:38.430 --> 00:03:43.370
You know, the interesting thing is that I
have relationships with a number of the Republicans

00:03:43.370 --> 00:03:45.599
that Trump is on top of my bills.

00:03:45.599 --> 00:03:50.920
We work on Yemen, but they will not, even
in private, criticize Trump.

00:03:50.920 --> 00:03:56.200
There's such a fear that that could get out
that someone could say something, that it

00:03:56.200 --> 00:03:57.200
could get back to Trump.

00:03:57.200 --> 00:04:03.010
So I have never had a Republican colleague,
even in private, in the House side at least

00:04:03.010 --> 00:04:08.599
say, oh, you know what, we need to move on
the very, very careful, very careful what

00:04:08.599 --> 00:04:09.599
they put in tax.

00:04:09.599 --> 00:04:14.110
Very careful what they would say to a to a
colleague when the when the topic is Donald

00:04:14.110 --> 00:04:15.380
Trump.

00:04:15.380 --> 00:04:20.629
So in terms of the Joe Biden cabinet, we're
learning about more and more of the picks.

00:04:20.629 --> 00:04:24.360
Maybe one of the more controversial ones is
Neera Tanden for OMB.

00:04:24.360 --> 00:04:26.720
We're going to talk about that tomorrow.

00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:30.349
We've seen Janet Yellen for Treasury, which
I think is actually very interesting.

00:04:30.349 --> 00:04:35.779
And she has some really interesting economic
literature and history that she's participated

00:04:35.779 --> 00:04:40.300
in over the last 30 years, which has been
interesting to look at the general idea of

00:04:40.300 --> 00:04:45.419
Bernie Sanders, Elizabeth Warren leaving the
Senate to be part of the Cabinet.

00:04:45.419 --> 00:04:52.460
Aside from specific positions strategically,
do you like it or not like it?

00:04:52.460 --> 00:04:54.569
I think they should be part of the government.

00:04:54.569 --> 00:04:58.770
At least one of them should be just because,
you know, we forget that Bernie Sanders won

00:04:58.770 --> 00:04:59.770
the first three states.

00:04:59.770 --> 00:05:04.860
I mean, I know you got the most delegates,
but he got the most votes in Iowa, New Hampshire,

00:05:04.860 --> 00:05:05.860
Nevada.

00:05:05.860 --> 00:05:06.860
He got millions of votes.

00:05:06.860 --> 00:05:13.650
And so to have that wing of the party not
represented, I think is a mistake.

00:05:13.650 --> 00:05:14.770
It's a mistake for Joe Biden.

00:05:14.770 --> 00:05:17.539
It's a mistake in terms of the progressive
energy.

00:05:17.539 --> 00:05:21.860
So Department of Labor is actually a place
he would make a big difference.

00:05:21.860 --> 00:05:23.029
I would support that.

00:05:23.029 --> 00:05:25.789
And it'll be a challenge if they both are
shut out.

00:05:25.789 --> 00:05:30.699
And my understanding is the Vermont governor
is committed to appointing an independent.

00:05:30.699 --> 00:05:33.059
It would caucus with Democrats.

00:05:33.059 --> 00:05:34.840
I also think Bernie Sanders has earned this.

00:05:34.840 --> 00:05:41.120
I mean, if he wants to continue to represent
folks in the Senate, that's his prerogative.

00:05:41.120 --> 00:05:45.830
But if he feels like he wants to make an impact
in the Labor Department, I don't see how with

00:05:45.830 --> 00:05:51.800
his success and all he represents, why this
is even a fight for him to get this position.

00:05:51.800 --> 00:05:56.749
So if the position of the Biden transition
team were simply we can't afford to lose them

00:05:56.749 --> 00:06:00.129
in the Senate, that would not satisfy you
as an explanation?

00:06:00.129 --> 00:06:04.849
No, because Bernie Sanders wouldn't give up
the Senate.

00:06:04.849 --> 00:06:09.270
And he has clearly gotten a commitment from
the Republican governor that they will appoint

00:06:09.270 --> 00:06:12.379
an independent there who will caucus with
the Democrats.

00:06:12.379 --> 00:06:17.710
So if if they were going to be blunt and said
we don't want Bernie Sanders for X, Y and

00:06:17.710 --> 00:06:20.900
Z reason, I think that would be more honest.

00:06:20.900 --> 00:06:28.610
My view is that it would be a mistake not
to give Bernie Sanders a platform where he

00:06:28.610 --> 00:06:30.830
thinks he can be most impactful.

00:06:30.830 --> 00:06:37.400
Bernie Sanders thinks he can do that in the
Department of Labor is he's earned that opportunity.

00:06:37.400 --> 00:06:42.129
Speaking of leaving the Senate, I mean, of
course, we have to mention that in your state,

00:06:42.129 --> 00:06:45.770
Kamala Harris is going to be the vice president
of the United States.

00:06:45.770 --> 00:06:48.129
And so that will be a seat that opens.

00:06:48.129 --> 00:06:53.729
I've read a little bit about who the sort
of front runners are.

00:06:53.729 --> 00:07:00.400
Your name is being mentioned by many progressives
as someone they would like to see Draft Rokan,

00:07:00.400 --> 00:07:05.009
call Gavin Newsom and say, is that is that
a position that would be interesting to you

00:07:05.009 --> 00:07:09.969
or anyone would be honored to to represent
the state in the Senate?

00:07:09.969 --> 00:07:15.650
And it would give me probably a bigger platform
to have an impact on progressive policies.

00:07:15.650 --> 00:07:19.139
But I believe I can also be very effective
from the House.

00:07:19.139 --> 00:07:24.139
And, you know, the leading candidate appears
to be Padia.

00:07:24.139 --> 00:07:27.309
My bigger issue is can we get someone?

00:07:27.309 --> 00:07:32.389
Is progressive, it was clearly for Medicare
for all, it was clearly for a 15 hour minimum

00:07:32.389 --> 00:07:37.919
wage, free public college or vocational education,
forgiving student loans.

00:07:37.919 --> 00:07:41.860
And how do we a policy and there are a number
of people who would fit that bill.

00:07:41.860 --> 00:07:43.449
But that, to me is the most important.

00:07:43.449 --> 00:07:44.449
Yeah.

00:07:44.449 --> 00:07:48.029
What's interesting, I like that you bring
that up because I wanted to talk about what

00:07:48.029 --> 00:07:54.330
is the strategy even with a 50 50 Senate with
Kamala Harris has a tiebreaker vote.

00:07:54.330 --> 00:07:58.509
There are lots of Democrats in the Senate
who are not on board with a lot of the ideas

00:07:58.509 --> 00:07:59.770
you just talked about.

00:07:59.770 --> 00:08:01.490
And so there's actually a real question.

00:08:01.490 --> 00:08:07.259
I mean, things like Medicare for all and and
many other ideas like Joe Manchin is not a

00:08:07.259 --> 00:08:09.639
guaranteed vote on a lot of those things.

00:08:09.639 --> 00:08:15.080
And we could go sort of down the list of some
of the more centrist, right leaning Democratic

00:08:15.080 --> 00:08:16.330
senators.

00:08:16.330 --> 00:08:17.629
Is that a concern to you?

00:08:17.629 --> 00:08:22.330
And thinking about the best case scenario
right now would be 50 50 with Kamala Harris

00:08:22.330 --> 00:08:23.800
is the tie breaker.

00:08:23.800 --> 00:08:27.300
It seems to me that even in that scenario,
there are real challenges in getting some

00:08:27.300 --> 00:08:29.919
of some of the things we'd like to see done
actually done.

00:08:29.919 --> 00:08:31.279
David, you're actually right.

00:08:31.279 --> 00:08:35.620
And this is where there is a philosophical
difference of what way the Obama administration

00:08:35.620 --> 00:08:36.620
should go.

00:08:36.620 --> 00:08:42.820
One theory of the case is let's solve the
debt limit, get the vaccine out, and then

00:08:42.820 --> 00:08:47.069
there's going to be some natural recovery
to the economy.

00:08:47.069 --> 00:08:52.389
And let's get whatever we can get in either
a Republican Senate or a divided Senate where

00:08:52.389 --> 00:08:55.180
everything has to get mansion's vote.

00:08:55.180 --> 00:08:56.670
I don't think that's sufficient.

00:08:56.670 --> 00:09:02.110
I think the better strategy is to say, here
is our vision.

00:09:02.110 --> 00:09:04.180
We've got record income inequality.

00:09:04.180 --> 00:09:06.009
We've got people who have been left out.

00:09:06.009 --> 00:09:11.069
We need to have massive public investment
in the creation of new jobs.

00:09:11.069 --> 00:09:15.199
We need to have massive investment in childcare
and education and health care.

00:09:15.199 --> 00:09:16.800
We're going to pass that in the House.

00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:18.269
We're going to build public support.

00:09:18.269 --> 00:09:20.420
We're going to make that case.

00:09:20.420 --> 00:09:25.110
And even if we're losing votes in the Senate,
eventually in twenty two, twenty four, twenty

00:09:25.110 --> 00:09:29.170
six and twenty eight, we're going to get that
just society.

00:09:29.170 --> 00:09:32.959
Any great leader in this country is not overnight
brought change.

00:09:32.959 --> 00:09:34.329
They built public support.

00:09:34.329 --> 00:09:37.720
We've seen what Bernie did from 2016 to now.

00:09:37.720 --> 00:09:43.269
So to surrender the effort at building public
support because it's hard or the numbers aren't

00:09:43.269 --> 00:09:48.029
there, I think is an abdication of political
imagination and political leadership.

00:09:48.029 --> 00:09:54.819
Yeah, I mean, speaking, if we apply a pragmatic
filter to that for a moment, we can imagine

00:09:54.819 --> 00:09:58.720
a situation where Democrats don't have the
Senate.

00:09:58.720 --> 00:10:06.290
And then the big things that Joe Biden campaigned
on, which are an expansion of Obamacare, public

00:10:06.290 --> 00:10:11.269
option, et cetera, pretty significant changes
to the tax code, including on capping Social

00:10:11.269 --> 00:10:14.740
Security beyond 400 K stepped up basis elimination.

00:10:14.740 --> 00:10:18.230
You know, we look at the full list of the
really big stuff.

00:10:18.230 --> 00:10:21.959
I'm hearing two things from from different
people on the left.

00:10:21.959 --> 00:10:23.279
And we just lost your video.

00:10:23.279 --> 00:10:24.279
I don't know.

00:10:24.279 --> 00:10:25.279
And you're back.

00:10:25.279 --> 00:10:26.279
There you go.

00:10:26.279 --> 00:10:27.279
That was right.

00:10:27.279 --> 00:10:30.041
So we're hearing two things from people on
the left and right on the left, rather, some

00:10:30.041 --> 00:10:35.640
on the left are saying you can't these Republicans
in the Senate cannot be compromised with what

00:10:35.640 --> 00:10:40.550
you would need to give up as Joe Biden if
you don't have the Senate to get any of those

00:10:40.550 --> 00:10:46.680
things done, would essentially degrade the
quality of the bill where Joe Biden would

00:10:46.680 --> 00:10:51.610
be giving huge concessions to the Republican
Party that are simply not worth doing.

00:10:51.610 --> 00:10:58.879
The other side is it's worth doing in order
to get anything at all, but it might end up

00:10:58.879 --> 00:11:01.270
being sort of a Pyrrhic victory.

00:11:01.270 --> 00:11:06.540
Do you have a strong feeling one way or the
other about how far it's worth negotiating

00:11:06.540 --> 00:11:09.500
with people who are often negotiating out
of such bad faith?

00:11:09.500 --> 00:11:16.180
Well, it depends on the context, I mean, if
it's a matter of getting some unemployment

00:11:16.180 --> 00:11:20.389
assistance to people and you know, where we
can get three hundred or four hundred dollars

00:11:20.389 --> 00:11:24.579
instead of six hundred dollars, I say we probably
have to compromise to get the relief.

00:11:24.579 --> 00:11:28.879
If we're talking about expanding Medicare,
if we're talking about free public college

00:11:28.879 --> 00:11:30.600
and if we're talking about student loan forgiveness.

00:11:30.600 --> 00:11:35.140
And there's really something, Tolkan, that
the Republicans are agreeing to, but they're

00:11:35.140 --> 00:11:39.519
asking for draconian cuts in other programs,
then?

00:11:39.519 --> 00:11:40.519
Absolutely not.

00:11:40.519 --> 00:11:44.120
What I would say is, let's pass in the House.

00:11:44.120 --> 00:11:47.600
Let's pass an advocate in the White House,
at least Biden's policies.

00:11:47.600 --> 00:11:52.939
I mean, let's at least make sure that that
they're passing then I don't see any justification

00:11:52.939 --> 00:11:58.850
for Joe Biden and Nancy Pelosi not passing
every single thing that he campaigned on.

00:11:58.850 --> 00:12:03.720
I mean, isn't that what prompted I mean, did
Trump it was wrong, but obviously went down

00:12:03.720 --> 00:12:04.720
this checklist.

00:12:04.720 --> 00:12:05.720
Here's what I campaigned on.

00:12:05.720 --> 00:12:06.930
Here's what I'm going to try to do.

00:12:06.930 --> 00:12:08.310
So Biden ought to do the same thing.

00:12:08.310 --> 00:12:10.019
We have a Democratic House.

00:12:10.019 --> 00:12:13.709
There's no reason why we shouldn't have every
single thing that he campaigned on up for

00:12:13.709 --> 00:12:14.709
a vote.

00:12:14.709 --> 00:12:18.079
And if we lose the vote, let people know which
Democrats voted against it, let people know

00:12:18.079 --> 00:12:23.020
who voted against the public option, who voted
against Medicare to 60, who voted against

00:12:23.020 --> 00:12:30.220
it, uncapping Social Security taxes over raising
having taxes, over four hundred thousand who

00:12:30.220 --> 00:12:34.999
voted against raising capital gains taxes,
over a million dollars.

00:12:34.999 --> 00:12:38.389
There are a lot of policies that we ought
to vote on.

00:12:38.389 --> 00:12:43.399
My fear is that they're going to say, oh,
let's have a lowest common denominator.

00:12:43.399 --> 00:12:49.620
Manchin, the moderate Democrats aren't for
it, that we're not going to have a vote and

00:12:49.620 --> 00:12:54.589
that's not going to fly, especially given
that we have the House last thing.

00:12:54.589 --> 00:12:57.089
Let's talk a little bit about what's going
on with the virus.

00:12:57.089 --> 00:13:03.120
We fortunately appear to be just a couple
of weeks from the first vaccine, starting

00:13:03.120 --> 00:13:08.720
to the first doses of the first vaccine, starting
to get to people December eight to 10.

00:13:08.720 --> 00:13:12.899
There's going to be this meeting to look at
least at the Pfizer emergency use authorization

00:13:12.899 --> 00:13:13.899
shortly thereafter.

00:13:13.899 --> 00:13:18.499
The Moderna one, we're told, within 24 to
48 hours of that, which could be December

00:13:18.499 --> 00:13:24.160
11th, 12th, 13th, the first doses are going
to start going into people's arms.

00:13:24.160 --> 00:13:26.480
There's two things that are sort of going
on here.

00:13:26.480 --> 00:13:32.899
One is the initially limited supply of these
vaccines where you can hopefully, best case,

00:13:32.899 --> 00:13:35.800
inoculate 20 million people a month.

00:13:35.800 --> 00:13:39.870
That's going to take a while before we get
to to to some level of herd immunity.

00:13:39.870 --> 00:13:44.389
The other side is 40 percent of the country
still says they're not really interested in

00:13:44.389 --> 00:13:48.950
getting a vaccine, which will make it very
difficult to ever get to that herd immunity.

00:13:48.950 --> 00:13:52.930
What are you and your colleagues looking at
and thinking about in terms of the vaccination

00:13:52.930 --> 00:13:53.930
program?

00:13:53.930 --> 00:13:57.680
Yeah, there was an interesting idea, actually,
by John Delaney, who sent it to the op ed

00:13:57.680 --> 00:14:02.660
to me, that he says that we ought to be linking
a fifteen hundred dollar stimulus or payment

00:14:02.660 --> 00:14:04.329
to getting the vaccine.

00:14:04.329 --> 00:14:09.360
Now, I haven't looked at all the constitutionality
or ethics around it, but I think having some

00:14:09.360 --> 00:14:16.529
sort of incentive system to get vaccine adoption
should be on the table.

00:14:16.529 --> 00:14:24.480
But at the very least, we need massive funding
on on outreach and figuring out how we incentivize

00:14:24.480 --> 00:14:27.499
hospitals to get the get it distributed.

00:14:27.499 --> 00:14:31.499
But I think it is it's going to be a challenge.

00:14:31.499 --> 00:14:36.010
I mean, you've got to get to about 60, 70
percent vaccination.

00:14:36.010 --> 00:14:38.639
And that's going to be the administrative
challenge.

00:14:38.639 --> 00:14:41.129
I will say this about Ron Klain.

00:14:41.129 --> 00:14:42.990
He's exceedingly competent.

00:14:42.990 --> 00:14:48.570
And whatever else you think about the Biden
administration, I do think he will manage

00:14:48.570 --> 00:14:49.570
this process.

00:14:49.570 --> 00:14:50.570
Well, yeah.

00:14:50.570 --> 00:14:54.839
I mean, the crazy thing is initially none
of these vaccines will be given to children.

00:14:54.839 --> 00:14:59.540
And once you remove children from the three
hundred and thirty million population, you

00:14:59.540 --> 00:15:04.529
actually need close to 100 percent of adults
to get to that 70 percent.

00:15:04.529 --> 00:15:07.970
And I don't think that that that is necessarily
being taken taken seriously.

00:15:07.970 --> 00:15:12.570
You're saying I haven't even thought about
that point.

00:15:12.570 --> 00:15:14.100
And it's a good point.

00:15:14.100 --> 00:15:16.839
I mean, look, this is where I think that the
Obama administration is.

00:15:16.839 --> 00:15:22.249
I mean, they're going to be judged in the
first six months to nine months on the administrative

00:15:22.249 --> 00:15:23.631
rollout of this vaccine.

00:15:23.631 --> 00:15:27.649
And I'm sure in the back of their minds is
that when the Affordable Care Act happened,

00:15:27.649 --> 00:15:33.740
it was botched the rollout, and that really
hurt people and it hurt the public perception.

00:15:33.740 --> 00:15:38.480
So they're right to be focused on that administration.

00:15:38.480 --> 00:15:40.149
But there is a dual crisis.

00:15:40.149 --> 00:15:43.050
There's that problem and then there's an economic
crisis.

00:15:43.050 --> 00:15:47.199
And then there's a structural crisis that
Elizabeth Warren and Bernie Sanders spoke

00:15:47.199 --> 00:15:53.220
about, in my view, is we can't ignore both
the structural crisis and the immediate crisis

00:15:53.220 --> 00:15:59.199
economically, and we have to pay attention
to that substantively and for political reasons.

00:15:59.199 --> 00:16:03.790
Do you like the idea of airlines, concert
venues, et cetera, saying after a certain

00:16:03.790 --> 00:16:07.839
date, if you want to come to an event or fly
with us, you must be vaccinated?

00:16:07.839 --> 00:16:11.880
Yes, I think that that's a very reasonable
position.

00:16:11.880 --> 00:16:13.889
And and and probably constitutional.

00:16:13.889 --> 00:16:18.370
I mean, my sense is that you are required
to have vaccinations to go to school, for

00:16:18.370 --> 00:16:19.439
example.

00:16:19.439 --> 00:16:25.139
So the conditioning being in public places,
it makes sense.

00:16:25.139 --> 00:16:29.619
You know, I was talking to a because I represent
Silicon Valley, one of the privileges I get

00:16:29.619 --> 00:16:33.620
to talk to some of these leading technologists
and scientists and talking to someone yesterday

00:16:33.620 --> 00:16:39.250
who said that if we put the resources, there's
no reason we couldn't have vaccine detectors

00:16:39.250 --> 00:16:44.199
like smoke detectors, which could literally
detect almost any vaccine in a room.

00:16:44.199 --> 00:16:50.019
And we put so much money in our military so
that we could detect the smallest threat to

00:16:50.019 --> 00:16:51.350
our country militarily.

00:16:51.350 --> 00:16:56.730
I don't understand why we're not putting in
the resources now to be detecting pandemics

00:16:56.730 --> 00:16:58.170
and future pandemics.

00:16:58.170 --> 00:16:59.170
That is interesting.

00:16:59.170 --> 00:17:02.470
That is not one I had heard, but I will certainly
look into that.

00:17:02.470 --> 00:17:08.720
We've been speaking with Congressman Roe,
representing California's 17th District.

00:17:08.720 --> 00:17:09.720
Always a pleasure having you on.

00:17:09.720 --> 00:17:10.720
David, thank you for having me on your show.

00:17:10.720 --> 00:17:12.589
The David Pakman Show at David Pakman Dotcom.

